===Determiner===
{{head|en|determiner|comparative|fewer|or|less|superlative|fewest|or|least}}

# {{lb|en|preceded by another determiner}} An [[indefinite]], but usually [[small]], [[number]] of.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|magazine={{w|The Economist}}
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21583270-new-zealands-plan-regulate-designer-drugs-better-trying-ban-them-and-failing-new A new prescription]
|passage=No sooner has a [synthetic] drug been blacklisted than chemists adjust their recipe and start churning out a subtly different one. These &ldquo;legal highs&rdquo; are sold for the '''few''' months it takes the authorities to identify and ban them, and then the cycle begins again.}}
#: ''There are a '''few''' cars ''(=''some, but a relatively small number'') ''in the street.''
#: ''Quite a '''few''' people '' (=''a significant number'') ''were pleasantly surprised.''
#: ''I think he's had a '''few''' drinks.'' [This usage is likely [[ironic]].]
# {{lb|en|used alone}} Not [[many]]; a small (in comparison with another number stated or implied) but somewhat indefinite number of.
#: {{ux|en|There are very '''few''' people who understand quantum theory.}}
#: ''I was expecting a big crowd at the party, but very '''few''' people ''(=''almost none'') ''turned up.''
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|1|III|iii|page=48|Your men are valiant but their number '''few''',<br>And cannot terrifie his mightie hoſt, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|meteorology|of clouds}} Obscuring one to two [[okta|oktas]] (eighths) of the sky.
#: {{ux|en|Tonight: A '''few''' clouds. Increasing cloudiness overnight.}}
#: [[NOAA]] definition of the term "few clouds": ''An official sky cover classification for aviation weather observations, descriptive of a sky cover of 1/8 to 2/8. This is applied only when obscuring phenomena aloft are present--that is, not when obscuring phenomena are surface-based, such as fog.''
# {{lb|en|meteorology|of rainfall with regard to a location}} ''(US?)'' Having a 10 percent chance of measurable precipitation (0.01 inch); used interchangeably with [[isolated]].

====Usage notes====
* {{m|en||Few}} is used with plural nouns only; its synonymous counterpart {{m|en|little}} is used with [[Appendix:Glossary#uncountable|uncountable]] nouns.

* Although indefinite in nature, ''a few'' is usually more than two (two often being referred to as "a couple of"), and less than "several". If the sample population is say between 5 and 20, ''a few'' would mean three or four, but no more than this. However, if the population sample size were in the millions, ''a few'' could refer to several hundred items. In other words, ''few'' in this context means ''a very very small percentage'' but far more than the 3 or 4 usually ascribed to it in its use with much much smaller numbers.

* {{m|en||Few}} is grammatically affirmative but semantically negative, and it can [[license]] [[negative polarity item]]s. For example, ''[[anything]]'' usually cannot be used in affirmative sentences, but can be used in sentences with {{m|en||few}}.

:* ''He didn't do anything to help us.''
:* *''He did anything to help us.'' (ungrammatical)
:* '''''Few''' people did anything to help us.''
:* *''A few people did anything to help us.'' (ungrammatical, since [[a few]] is a different unit of meaning from ''few'' and does not license NPIs)

* ''Few'' alone emphasises smallness of number, while ''a few'' emphasises ''some''. For example: ''He's a dull man with '''few''' ideas''; ''He's a clever man with '''a few''' ideas.''

